## Chapter 1 Introduction

### 1.1 Standard Model and Quantum Chromodynamics

#### Standard Model

The Standard Model of particle physics is a theory of fundamental particles and how they interact. It was formulated in the \(1970\)s. Since then, discoveries of the \(W\) and \(Z\) bosons in \(1983\), the top quark in \(1995\), the tau neutrino in \(2000\), and the Higgs boson in \(2012\), have given further credence to the Standard Model.

According to this model, all matter is built from a small number of fundamental spin \(1/2\) particles, or fermions: six quarks (up, down, charm, strange, top, bottom) and six leptons (electron, electron neutrino, muon, muon neutrino, tau, tau neutrino). Fermions respect the Pauli exclusion principle according to the spin-statistics theorem. Each fermion has a corresponding antiparticle. Quarks carry color charge and interact via the strong interaction. Quarks cannot exist as free particles and are very strongly bound to one another, forming color-neutral composite particles (hadrons) This phenomenon is called color confinement. Quarks also carry electric charge and weak isospin. Hence they interact with other fermions both electromagnetically and via the weak interaction.

Leptons do not carry color charge. The three neutrinos do not carry electric charge either. Their motion is directly influenced only by the weak nuclear force. Particles exhibiting similar physical behavior are grouped together to form a generation (Fig. 1.1).

The first generation charged particles do not decay. The common material of the present universe is made of such particles. Second and third generation charged particles decay with very short life time and are observed only in very high-energy environments. Neutrinos of all generations also do not decay. They rarely interact with baryonic matter.

The Standard Model also comprises interactions between particles. The different interactions are described in quantum language in terms of the exchange of characteristic bosons between the fermion constituents. There are four types of fundamental interaction or field. Strong interactions are responsible for binding the quarks in the neutron and proton, and the neutrons and protons within nuclei. The interquark force is mediated by a massless particle, the gluon. Electromagnetic interactions are responsible for virtually all the phenomena in in extra-nuclear physics and for the intermolecular forces in liquids and solids. The photon is massless and is well-described by the theory of quantum electrodynamics. Weak interactions between particles of different flavors are mediated by the \(W^{+}\), \(W^{-}\) and \(Z\) gauge bosons. They are massive, with masses of order \(100\) times the proton mass. Gravitational interactions act between all types ofparticle. Gravity is by far the weakest of all the fundamental interactions on the scale of experiments in particle physics, although it is dominant on the scale of the universe. It is supposedly mediated by exchanged of a spin \(2\) boson, the graviton.

#### Quantum Chromodynamics

In theoretical physics, quantum chromodynamics (QCD) is the theory of strong interactions, a fundamental force describing the interactions between quarks and gluons which make up hadrons such as the proton, neutron and pion. As part of the standard model, mathematically, the theory is a non-Abelian gauge theory based on a local symmetry group \(SU(3)\). The QCD analog of electric charge is a property called color. The force carrier of the theory are gluons, like photons are for the electromagnetic force in quantum electrodynamics. The QCD Lagrangian has the form

\[{\cal L}_{QCD}=\sum_{f}i\bar{\psi}_{f,k}\gamma^{\mu}D_{\mu}\psi_{f}-m_{f}\bar{ \psi}_{f,k}\psi_{f,k}-\frac{1}{4}F^{i}_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu,i}\;, \tag{1}\]

\[D_{\mu}\psi=(\partial_{\mu}-igA^{\alpha}_{\mu}T_{\alpha})\psi\;, \tag{2}\]

\[F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_{\mu}A_{\mu}-\partial_{\nu}A_{\nu}-ig[A_{\mu},A_{\nu}]\;, \tag{3}\]

Figure 1: The standard Model of elementary particles, with the three generations of matter, gauge bosons in the fourth column, and the Higgs boson in the fifth. Figure taken from Wikipedia.

Where \(f\) is the flavor index (labels up, down, strange, charm, top, and bottom), \(k\) is the color index, \(D_{\mu}\) is the covariant derivative, \(F_{\mu\nu}\) is the field strength tensor, \(A_{\nu}\) and \(A_{\mu}\) are the gauge field operators, \(m_{f}\) is the quark mass, and \(g\) is the coupling constant.

QCD enjoys two peculiar properties: asymptotic freedom and confinement.

Asymptotic freedomAsymptotic freedom is the property that the interaction between particles becomes weaker at shorter distances. This prediction of QCD was first discovered in the early 1970s by David Politzer, Frank Wilczek and David Gross [15, 16]. One might expect the static QCD potential to have a similar form of \(1/r\) to that in QED since gluons are massless. In fact the quark-antiquark potential is often taken to be of the form

\[V_{s}=-\frac{4}{3}\frac{\alpha_{s}}{r}+kr\, \tag{4}\]

Where the first term arises from single-gluon exchange and dominates at small \(r\). It is similar to the Coulomb potential between elementary charges. The second linear term is associated with confinement at large \(r\). Because fo the linear term, attempts to free a quark from a hadron simply result in the production of new \(Q\bar{Q}\) pairs, i.e. mesons.

The effective QCD coupling constant \(\alpha_{s}=g_{s}^{2}/4\pi\) can be written as

\[\alpha_{s}(\mu)\approx\frac{4\pi}{\beta_{0}\ln\left(\mu^{2}/\Lambda_{QCD}^{2} \right)}\, \tag{5}\]

Where \(\beta_{0}\) is a constant dependent on the number of quarks with mass less than \(\mu\), \(\mu\) is momentum transfer scale and \(\Lambda_{QCD}\) is called the QCD scale which needs to be determined experimentally.

Fig. 2 shows QCD effective coupling \(\alpha_{s}\) as a function of momentum transfer scale. Due to the running coupling, QCD uses different methods at different energy scale. \(\alpha_{s}\to 0\) when \(\mu\to\infty\). Therefore QCD (pQCD) can be calculated perturbatively in high momentum transfer or short distance approach. The coupling is very strong at \(\mu\sim\Lambda_{QCD}\). In this case, QCD is non-perturbative. Other methods are used such as Lattice QCD and AdS/CFT.

ConfinementAnother striking feature of QCD is the color confinement which is the other side of the asymptotic freedom [17, 18, 19]. This is the phenomenon that color charged particles (such as quarks and gluons) cannot be isolated as separate objects. Because of this, when you do separate a quark from other quarks, the energy in the gluon field is enough to create another quark pair; they are thus forever bound into hadrons such as the proton and the neutron or the pion and kaon. Although analytically unproven, confinement is widely believed to be true because it explains the consistent failure of free quark searches, and it is easy to demonstrate in lattice QCD.

### 1.2 Quark Gluon Plasma and Relativistic Heavy Ion Collisions

#### Quark Gluon Plasma

In quantum chromodynamics, quark gluon plasma (QGP) is a state of matter which is hypothesized to exist at extremely high temperature, density, or both temperature and density. This state is thought to consist of asymptotically free quarks and gluons, which are several of the basic building blocks of matter. It is believed that up to a few milliseconds after the Big Bang, known as the Quark epoch, the Universe was in a quark gluon plasma state.

At low energy densities, quarks and gluons are confined in hadrons and chiral symmetry is broken. However, with increasing temperature and/or increasing baryon density, a phase transition may occur to the state where the ordinary hadrons do not exist anymore, and where quarks and gluons become the correct degrees of freedom and chiral symmetry is restored [20]. Fig. 1.3 shows phase diagram of nuclear matter of parameters temperature (\(T\)) and baryon chemical potential (\(\mu_{B}\)). At baryon chemical potential equal to zero (\(\mu_{B}=0\)), finite temperature Lattice QCD calculations suggest a smooth cross-over above a critical point temperature \(T_{c}\sim\)\(170\)-\(190\) MeV [21, 22] from

