#### Relativistic Heavy Ion Collisions

According to the phase diagram calculated by Lattice QCD, there are generally two ways to create the quark gluon plasma. One is to increase the temperature [27, 28], the other is to increase the baryon number density. Ultra-relativistic heavy ion collisions were proposed as a means to create the quark gluon plasma in the first way.

In the ultra-relativistic heavy ion collisions, the energy per nucleon in the center-of-mass frame is much larger than the nucleon mass. At such large energies, two colliding nuclei travel almost at the speed of light and are Lorentz contracted. Fig. 1.4 shows QGP space-time evolution. In the center-of-mass frame, partons moving fast hadronize later than those moving slowly. Therefore, the evolution of the system at midrapidity is governed by the longitudinal proper time \(\tau=\sqrt{t^{2}-z^{2}}\), rather than by the ordinary time \(t\).

The thermalization/equilibration time is very short, \(\tau_{therm}\sim\leq 1\) fm/c. A locally

Figure 1.3: Phase diagram of nuclear matter.

thermalized quark gluon plasma is created after the thermalization. The subsequent evolution of the system may be described by the equations of the relativistic perfect-fluid hydrodynamics. The temperature decreases as the system expands. When the temperature drops below the phase transition critical value (\(T_{c}\)), the system goes back into a hadronic state. The hadrons interact with each other via inelastic and elastic collisions. The inelastic cross sections are typically smaller than the elastic cross section. As the hadronic system cools down the inelastic collisions between its constituents are very likely to cease before the elastic collisions. The moment when the inelastic collisions stop is defined as the chemical freeze-out. Hadron species become fixed after chemical freeze-out. As the system further expands, the mean free path of hadrons increases. The thermal or kinetic freeze-out is the stage in the evolution of matter when the hadrons practically stop to interact. The momentum distributions of particles are frozen after thermal freeze-out. Naturally, the temperatures corresponding to the two freeze-outs satisfy the condition \(T_{ch}>T_{fo}\). Where \(T_{ch}\) is the temperature of the chemical freeze-out and \(T_{fo}\) is the temperature of the thermal freeze-out.

### 1.3 Anisotropic flow

In non-central collisions the overlap region of two colliding nuclei has an almond shape in the transverse plane as show in Fig. 1.5.

Figure 1.4: QGP space-time evolution. Figure taken from [2]

A vector connecting centers of the colliding nuclei in the transverse plane is called the impact vector \(\vec{b}\). The length of the impact vector is the impact parameter. We introduce a coordinate system, where the spatial \(z\)-axis is parallel to the beam direction, and where the impact vector \(\vec{b}\) points the \(x\)-direction. The \(x\) and \(z\) axes span the reaction plane (RP) of a given collision. The pressure gradient along the \(x\) axis is larger than that along the \(y\) axis. Because the expansion of the system is drove by the pressure gradient, particles will gain a larger momentum along the \(x\) axis than along the \(y\) axis. The spatial anisotropy is thus transfered into a momentum anisotropy.

Event-by-event, the single particle azimuthal distribution with respect to the reaction plane can be written in a form of Fourier series [29]

\[dN/d\phi\propto 1+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}2v_{n}\cos n(\phi-\Psi_{n})\, \tag{6}\]

where \(\Psi_{n}\) is the \(n^{th}\) order harmonic reaction plane. The Fourier coefficient \(v_{n}\) stands for the \(n\)-th harmonic of the event azimuthal anisotropy. We can get the \(n\)-th harmonic coefficient \(v_{n}\) as

\[v_{n}=\langle\cos n(\phi-\Psi_{n})\rangle\, \tag{7}\]

The first order harmonic \(v_{1}\) is referred as directed flow, and the second order harmonic

Figure 5: Distribution of nucleons in the transverse plane generated in a Glauber Monte Carlo simulation. The outlines of the two nuclei are shown as circles. The overlap region of two nuclei has an almond shape. Wounded nucleons (participants) are indicated as black solid circles, while spectators are light dotted circles. Figure taken from [3].

is referred as \(v_{2}\). The \(v_{n}\) anisotropies result in a modulation of two-particle distributions in relative azimuthal angle

\[dN/d\Delta\phi\propto 1+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}2\left\langle v_{n}^{trig}v_{n}^{assoc} \right\rangle\text{cos}\,n\Delta\phi\, \tag{8}\]

The reaction plane can be estimated using the anisotropic flow itself. It means that the event plane can be determined independently for each harmonic of the anisotropic flow. The estimated angle is called the event plane (EP) to emphasize the fact that it is an experimental estimate of the reaction plane with finite resolution. The event plane angle \(\Psi_{n}\) from the \(n\)th harmonic of the distribution is given by [29]

\[Q_{n}\,\text{cos}(n\Psi_{n})=X_{n}=\sum_{i}w_{i}\,\text{cos}(n\phi_{i}) \tag{9}\]

\[Q_{n}\,\text{sin}(n\Psi_{n})=Y_{n}=\sum_{i}w_{i}\,\text{sin}(n\phi_{i}) \tag{10}\]

\[\Psi_{n}=\left(\text{tan}^{-1}\,\frac{\sum_{i}w_{i}\,\text{sin}(n\phi_{i})}{ \sum_{i}w_{i}\,\text{cos}(n\phi_{i})}\right)/n \tag{11}\]

The sums go over the all the particles used in the event plane determination. The \(w_{i}\) are weights to determine the event plane with the best resolution.

Directed flowDirected flow refers to collective sidewards deflection of particles [29]. It is characterized by a first-order harmonic (\(v_{1}\)) of the Fourier expansion of particle's azimuthal distribution with respect to the reaction plane. Directed flow is believed to be generated during the nuclear passage time (\(2R/\gamma\sim 0.1\) fm/c) [30, 31] before the thermalization happens, thus, it carries early information from the collision. At the SPS energies, the proton directed flow is positive at positive rapidities, while the pion directed flow is negative [32]. This suggests a different origin of \(v_{1}\) of protons and pions. STAR measures directed flow for charged particles in Au+Au and Cu+Cu collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 200 and 62.4 GeV, as a function of pseudorapidity (\(\eta\)), transverse momentum (\(p_{T}\)), and collision centrality. The directed flow depends on the incident energy but, not on the size of the colliding system at a given centrality. Fig. 6 shows the directed flow \(v_{1}\) of charged particles measured by STAR for three centralities in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 200 GeV. The directed flow of charged particles is negative, whereas the \(v_{1}\) of the spectator neutrons is positive. This trend suggests different behavior of the matter created in the central region and in the target/projectile fragmentation regions. STAR's measurements of directed flow (\(v_{1}\)) around midrapidity for \(\pi^{\pm}\), \(K^{\pm}\), \(K^{0}_{S}\), \(p\) and \(\bar{p}\) in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 200 GeV are presented at [33].

Elliptic flowThe elliptic flow \(v_{2}\) characterizes the azimuthal asymmetry of the momentum distributions. \(v_{2}\) is negative at lower energies, indicating that more momentum is transfered in the direction perpendicular to the reaction plane. The elliptic flow becomes positive at higher energies, more particles are emitted in-plane than out-of-plane. This energy dependence is interpreted as the effect of spectators. The spectators block the expansion of matter in the reaction plane at lower energies. The matter is squeezed out of the reaction plane. At higher energies the spectators move sufficiently fast, leaving free space for the in-plane expansion of matter. Measurements of the elliptic flow at RHIC [34] showed a sizable positive \(v_{2}\) in mid-central Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 200 GeV/c about 6 percent for particles within \(0.15<p_{T}<2.0\) GeV/c. Interaction between particles transfer the initial spatial anisotropy in the non-central collisions into the anisotropy in momentum space.

Higher order flow harmonicsThe elliptic flow \(v_{2}\) is expected to be the dominant term due to the elliptic shape of the overlap region between the two colliding nuclei. Higher order even harmonics were thought to be small compared to \(v_{2}\) and odd harmonics were thought to be zero because of the symmetry of the system [9]. It has not been realized until recently [3, 35] that such symmetry does not exist on event-by

