event basis because of quantum fluctuations in initial nucleon distributions in nuclei. Event-by-event fluctuations in the initial collision geometry leads to higher order flow harmonics through the collective expansion of the medium. Fig. 1.5 shows the initial positions of nucleons in an event generated with a Glauber Monte Carlo. Clear nonzero triangularity is observed.

Fig. 1.7 shows \(v_{n}\) results at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=200\) GeV from PHENIX [6] and STAR [7] as compared to theoretical calculations from the IP-Glasma+MUSIC model [8]. Data show the connection between odd harmonics and initial state fluctuations by comparing \(v_{3}\) measured with respect to the third order event plane to hydrodynamic calculations. Measurements of the correlations between event planes in both sides of the collision region provide additional evidence for the connection between \(v_{n}\) and the initial state fluctuations.

Fig. 1.8 shows raw correlation strength \(\langle\cos(j[\Psi_{n}^{A}-\Psi_{m}^{B}])\rangle\) and \(\langle\cos(j[\Psi_{n}^{C}-\Psi_{m}^{D}])\rangle\) of the event planes for various detector combinations as a function of collisions centrality from PHENIX [6]. Expected correlation between \(\Psi_{1}\) and \(\Psi_{2}\) as well as that between \(\Psi_{2}\) and \(\Psi_{4}\) are confirmed by Fig. 1.8(c). By contrast, Fig. 1.8(d) shows that there is no significant correlation observed between \(\Psi_{2}\) and \(\Psi_{3}\). The absence of this correlation

