### 4 Jet-medium Interactions

Large transverse momentum (\(p_{T}\)) partons in the high-density system result from the initial hard scattering of nucleon constituents [36]. After a hard scattering, the parton fragments to create a high-energy cluster (jet) of particles. A high-momentum parton traversing a dense colored medium is predicted to experience substantial energy loss [37, 38] and may be absorbed. High-\(p_{T}\) particle production is strongly biased towards surface of the collision zone. The study of single particle production at high \(p_{T}\) gives limited information about the interior of the QCD medium and about jet-medium interactions. However, the away-side jet partner opposite to a high-\(p_{T}\) particle traverses the entire volume suffering maximal interactions with the medium. The primary goal of di-hadron and multi-hadron correlations with high \(p_{T}\) trigger particles is to study the energy loss mechanisms due to interactions of energetic partons with the QGP medium. The near- and away-side peaks of jet-like correlations in \(p\)+\(p\) collisions are both approximately Gaussian. The away-side correlations in central heavy-ion collisions are

Figure 8: Raw correlation strength between event planes as measured in various detector combinations as a function of collisions centrality from PHENIX [6]. Panels (a) and (b) show the two sub-event correlations for \(m=n\); (c) and (d) show the two sub-event correlations for \(m\neq n\).

significantly broadened, even double-peaked [39, 40, 41, 42, 43, 44, 45]. There are many theoretical proposals for the production mechanisms of the away-side double-peak correlations. These models can broadly divided into two categories [9]. One is microscopic interactions of jets with flowing medium partons. The other is collective medium excitation by Mach cone shock waves caused by energy deposition of jets in the medium. One thing to keep in mind is that these theoretical models were proposed before the realization of hydrodynamic triangular flow. The jet-like correlation measurements mentioned above only subtract a background of elliptic flow \(v_{2}\). The away-side broadened and double-peaked correlations may be mainly caused by an additional background of triangular flow \(v_{3}\).

#### Mach-cone shock waves

An energetic particle deposits energy in a medium and produces medium excitations. These excitations are propagated within the limited region of a Mach cone [46, 47, 48, 49, 50, 51] when the energetic particle travels faster than the speed of sound of the medium. Decay of these excitations would result in double-peaked distributions of correlated particles.

A wide range of calculations of isolated energetic partons interacting with the QGP medium indicates the possibility of Mach cone shock waves. These calculations can be generally grouped into two categories. One is hydrodynamic calculations with source terms describing parton energy loss with linear response approximation [52, 53, 54]. The other utilizes AdS/CFT correspondence which can only calculate effects of heavy quark energy loss [55, 56, 57, 58, 59, 60]. Both collisional and radiative energy loss mechanisms can generate Mach cone shock waves [61].

Mach cone is shown to be unlikely observable in final-state hadrons after hadronization. Even before hadronization, multiple sources of energy deposition make well-defined formation of Mach cones unlikely [62]. In addition, the collective flow of the QGP itself will likely make observation of the Mach cone difficult [63]. Other jet-related physics mechanisms [64, 65, 66, 67] besides Mach cone shock waves have been proposed which can mimic a Mach cone like signal.

#### Effects of medium on jets

Jets deflected by medium flow event-by-event [68, 69] can also generate the double-peak structure. Such conclusion is also reached by hydrodynamic study of jet-induced medium excitation with a realistic source term for energy-momentum deposition [53, 70].

Three-particle correlation data [10, 71] have shown an evidence of deflected jets. In the deflected jet scenario, particles from the away-side jet stay relatively close while their angles vary over a wide range event-by-event. Fig. 1.9 shows the STAR three-particle correlation result in central Au+Au correlations. With the non-zero hydrodynamic odd harmonics, the off-diagonal (\(\Delta\phi_{1}+\Delta\phi_{2}=2\pi\)) three-particle correlation peaks are no longer unique signature of Mach-cone shock waves. Higher-order harmonics especially triangular flow \(v_{3}\) need to be subtracted in order to draw conclusions regarding Mach-cone shock waves. Fig. 1.9 also shows that the away-side diagonal (\(\Delta\phi_{1}=\Delta\phi_{2}\)) peak strengths are stronger that the off-diagonal strengths. Since triangular flow produce diagonal and off-diagonal peaks with equal-strength. This may be evidence of deflected jets on the away side of the trigger particle.

