## Chapter 2 Experimental Setup

### 2.1 Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider

The Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider (RHIC) is a versatile collider located at Brookhaven National Lab in Upton, New York on long Island. RHIC has successfully collided proton and proton (\(p\)+\(p\)), proton and aluminium (\(p\)+Al), proton and gold (\(p\)+Au), deuteron and gold (\(d\)+Au), helium \(3\) and gold (\({}^{3}\)He+Au), copper and copper (Cu+Cu), copper and gold (Cu+Au), gold and gold (Au+Au) and uranium and uranium (U+U) at different energies since year 2000. The top center-of-mass collision energy is \(200\) GeV per nucleon pair for heavy ion collisions and \(500\) GeV for polarized \(p\)+\(p\) collisions. A beam energy scan program for Au+Au collisions at center-of-mass energy from \(7.7\) GeV to \(200\) GeV per nucleon pair has been conducted [72, 73, 74, 75]. The two major physics programs at RHIC are spin physics, using polarized protons, and heavy ion physics. Reviews on the spin physics program can be found in references [76].

Fig. 2.1 shows RHIC accelerator complex. RHIC accelerator complex comprises the Tandem van de Graaff pre-accelerator, a linear proton accelerator, the Booster Synchrotron, the Alternating Gradient Synchrotron (AGS) and the RHIC synchrotron ring.

Figure 2.1: RHIC accelerator complex.

There are six interaction points and on RHIC's long storage ring whose circumference is \(3.8\) km. \(4\) of the interaction points are equipped with detectors. They are STAR at \(6\) o'clock, PHENIX at 8 o'clock, PHOBOS at 10 o'clock, and BRAHMS at 1 o'clock, respectively.

Fig. 2.2 shows RHIC acceleration scenario for Au beams. Negatively charged Au ions from the Pulsed Sputter Ion Source are injected into the Tandem Van de Graaff and are partially stripped of their electrons. They exit with an energy of \(1\) MeV/nucleon and a charge state of \(+32\). The ions are further stripped to a charge stat of \(+77\) in the Booster Synchrotron and accelerated to \(95\) MeV/nucleon. Then they are delivered into the AGS. The ions are fully stripped with a charge state of \(+79\) and are accelerated to \(10.8\) GeV/nucleon before transferred into the RHIC storage rings through the AGS-to-RHIC Beam Transfer Line.

RHIC also has the capability of accelerating polarized proton beams. Protons are injected from the \(200\) MeV Linac into the booster, followed by acceleration in the AGS and injection into RHIC. To study the partonic structure of nuclei, RHIC has been proposed as a possible facility for a future electron-ion collider.

Figure 2.2: RHIC acceleration scenario for Au beams.

### 2.2 STAR Experiment

The Solenoidal Tracker at RHIC (STAR) is one of two large detector systems constructed at the Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider (RHIC) at Brookhaven National Laboratory. STAR was constructed to investigate the behavior of strongly interacting matter at high energy density and to search for signatures of quark gluon plasma (QGP) formation. A large number of produced particles (up to approximately one thousand per unit pseudo-rapidity) and high momentum particles from hard parton-parton scattering are the key features of the nuclear environment at RHIC. Many observables will be measured simultaneously by STAR to study signatures of a possible QGP phase transition and to understand the space-time evolution of the collision process in ultra-relativistic heavy ion collisions. The goal is to obtain a fundamental understanding of the microscopic structure of these hadronic interactions at high energy densities. In order to accomplish this, STAR was designed primarily for charged hadron production measurements with high precision tracking and momentum over a large solid angle at the center of mass (c.m.) rapidity. The large acceptance of STAR makes it particularly well suited for event-by-event characterizations of heavy ion collisions.

Fig. 2.3 shows perspective view of the STAR detector. STAR is a massive detector weighing \(1200\) tons and as big as a building. It has a relatively uniform acceptance in azimuthal angle, and also a large polar angle coverage in mid-rapidity. It consists of several sub-systems. The whole detector is surrounded by the main magnetic coil which generates a field along the beam pipe direction with the maximum of \(|B_{z}|=0.5\) T.

The standard STAR coordinate system uses the center of the Time Projection Chamber as the origin point. The direction \(x\) is pointing to the south and away from center of the RHIC accelerator. The direction \(y\) is pointing opposite the direction of gravity. The beam pipe direction is the \(z\) direction with the west direction as being positive.

#### Time Projection Chamber

The Time Projection Chamber (TPC) is the primary tracking device in the STAR detector [11]. It provides tracking, momentum measurement, and particle identifications. The momentum can be determined from the curvature of the tracks in the magnetic field. The particle identifications is accomplished by measuring their ionization energy loss (\(dE/dx\)). TPC covers \(2\pi\) in azimuth and pseudo-rapidity \(|\eta|<1.8\).

Fig. 2.4 shows the schematic cutaway view of STAR TPC detector. The TPC sits in the middle of the STAR solenoidal magnet system which provides a \(0.5\) T uniform magnetic field. It is \(4.2\) m long along the beam line and has inner and outer diameters of \(1\) and \(4\) m, respectively. The TPC volume is filled with P10 gas (\(90\)% argon and\(10\)% methane) regulated at 2 mbar above atmospheric pressure. The gas is maintained to a high purity by the TPC gas system. A well defined, uniform electric field of \(\approx 135\) V/cm is applied to the TPC which is what causes the electrons to drift to the ends. The primary advantage of P10 gas is that electrons has a fast drift velocity which peaks at a low electric field. Operating on the peak of the velocity curve makes the drift velocity stable and insensitive to small variations in temperature and pressure. Uniformity of the electric field is crucial to achieve submillimeter track reconstruction precision for electron drift paths of up to 2.1 m. The uniform electric field in TPC is provided by a thin high voltage conductive Central Membrane (CM) at the center of the TPC, the inner and outer concentric field-cage cylinders and the readout end caps on the ends of the TPC. The Central Membrane is a cathode operating at a potential of \(-28\) kV and the end caps are at ground. The field cages keep the electric field uniform and provide containment for the gas. The typical drift velocity of electrons in the STAR TPC is \(5.45\) cm/\(\mu\)s. The transverse diffusion (\(\sigma=\sqrt{2Dt}\) where \(D\) is the diffusion coefficient and \(t\) is the drift time) of the drifting electrons is \(230\)\(\mu m/\sqrt{cm}\) with \(140\) V/cm drift field and 0.5 T magnetic field. For an electron drifting \(2.1\) m this gives a transverse diffusion of \(3.3\) mm. The longitudinal diffusion is \(360\)\(\mu\)m/\(\sqrt{cm}\). The diffusion sets the scale for the readout.

The readout system is based on Multi-Wire Proportional Chambers (MWPC) with

