#### Zero Degree Calorimeters

The RHIC zero degree calorimeters (ZDCs) [12] are located at \(\pm 18\) m away from the geometry center of the STAR detector. Fig. 2.8 shows the schematic diagram of the ZDC. High energy collisions of nuclei usually lead to the emission of evaporation neutrons from both "beam" and "target" nuclei. Evaporation neutrons diverge by less than 2 mrad from the beam axis at the RHIC heavy ion collider with \(100\) GeV/nucleon. Neutral beam fragments can be detected downstream of RHIC ion collisions if \(\theta\leq 4\) mrad while charged fragments in the same angular range are usually too close to the beam trajectory. Energy deposited by produced particles and other secondaries in this 'zero degree' region is negligible when compared with that of beam fragmentation neutrons. The purpose of the ZDC is to measure the energy deposition of neutrons, which are associated with the spectator.

The ZDC coincidence of the two beam directions is a minimal bias selection of heavy ion collisions. This makes it useful as an event trigger and a luminosity monitor. The ZDCs together with the Central Trigger Barrel (CTB) [77] characterize the collision centrality. The CTB is arranged around the TPC and has similar phase space as the TPC. The ZDC signal is small in peripheral Au+Au collisions because there are only a few neutron spectators and the gold ions are largely untouched and bent away by the dipole magnet. Meanwhile, the CTB has a small signal since the number of charged particles produced from the collision is also small. In mid-central Au+Au collisions, the gold ion spectators are mostly shattered into nucleons. The maximum number of neutron spectators is reached. The ZDC thus receives its maximum signal. The particle multiplicity at mid-rapidity for the CTB coverage is intermediate between central

Figure 2.8: The schematic diagram of the ZDC. Figure taken from [12].

and peripheral events. In central Au+Au collisions, the maximum multiplicity at mid-rapidity gives the maximum CTB signal. However, the number of neutron spectators again becomes small since all the nucleons participate in the interaction. This results in a small ZDC signal. The ZDC-measured neutral energy versus the CTB-measured charged particle multiplicity has a arch shape. The ZDC and CTB signals together can give the collision centrality information in Au+Au collisions.

#### The TOFp/pVPD time-of-flight system

A time-of-flight system was constructed for the direct identification of hadrons produced in Au+Au collisions for the STAR Experiment at RHIC. The system consists of two separate detector subsystems [78]. The "start" detector is called the Pseudo Vertex Position Detector (pVPD). The "stop" detector is called the Time of Flight Patch (TOFp). These detectors are based on conventional scintillator/phototube technology. Fig. 2.9 shows a scale drawing of the locations of pVPD and TOFp detectors in relation to the STAR TPC and the RHIC beam pipe. Charged Particle Identification (PID) in STAR is possible via \(dE/dx\) in the TPC. TPC can provide PID information over a momenta range of \(0.1\lesssim p\lesssim 0.7\) GeV/c (\(0.1\lesssim p\lesssim 1.0\) GeV/c) for kaons (protons). The STAR time-of-flight system with a \(100\) ps total timing resolution provide direct \(\pi/K/p\) identification up to momenta of \(\sim 1.7\) (\(1.9\)) GeV/c, and direct p versus (\(\pi\)+\(K\)) identification up to \(\sim 2.6\) (\(3.1\)) GeV/c, for tracks near \(\eta\sim 0\) (1). Deuterons can be directly identified out to \(\sim 4\) (\(4.7\)) GeV/c. VPD can also be used as fast detectors to trigger on the minimum bias events in Au+Au collisions. Vertex \(Z\) (\(V_{z}\)) is defined as the \(z\) position of the primary vertex along the beam direction. The vpdVz is determined by the vertex position detector (vpd) with good timing. A good reconstructed primary vertex from TPC tracks should be close to the vpdVz. Applying a vertex difference cut basically is able to reject those pile up vertices far away from the real vertex.

