## Chapter 3 Two-particle azimuthal correlations

### 3.1 Introduction and Motivation

Jets are quenched in relativistic heavy ion collisions due to interactions in the dense medium [81, 82, 83, 84, 85, 36, 86]. Jet-quenching can be measured via jet-like dihadron correlations. Measurements of jet medium modifications have so far been obscured because of the large underlying anisotropic flow background.

Fig. 3.1 shows azimuthal angle difference distributions for trigger particle \(4.0<p_{T}^{trig}<6.0\) GeV/c and associated particles with \(1.0<p_{T}^{assoc}<2.5\) GeV/c in \(0\)-\(12\)% central Au+Au collisions [14]. The associated hadron distribution contains a background of uncorrelated particles due to the correlations of particles with the event plane. The background is modeled using the function \(B[1+2\langle v_{2}^{trig}\rangle\langle v_{2}^{assoc}\rangle\cos(2\Delta\phi)]\). The subtraction of the elliptic flow \(v_{2}\) background involves determination of the elliptic flow parameters \(v_{2}\) and the normalization \(B\). Elliptic flow parameters \(v_{2}\) are measured using the reaction plane method with the forward TPC and the four-particle cumulant method [87]. These two methods have different sensitivity to nonflow effects and flow fluctuations and the difference between them is used as the estimate of the systematic uncertainty in \(v_{2}\). The elliptic flow background has been normalized to the data around \(|\Delta\phi|=1\) assuming that the background-subtracted signal is zero at minimum. This is referred to as the ZYAM (zero yield at minimum) method [88]. Those measurements [14, 39] are usually coming with large uncertainties. The method of normalizing the combinatorial background level in the region around \(|\Delta\phi|=1\) was first used for di-hadron correlations at higher momenta [89, 90], where the peaks on the near and away sides are narrow and well separated. However, the correlation peaks are much broader at lower \(p_{T}\). The separation between flow and nonflow in the analysis of azimuthal correlations is vague which makes the background normalization more ambiguous. Additionally, there is a larger combinatorial background at lower \(p_{T}\). The elliptic flow background is of similar size as the trigger-associated-hadron correlation signal.

After subtracting a background of elliptic flow \(v_{2}\), di-hadron correlations are studied for different centralities and trigger particle \(p_{T}\) ranges. Jet-like correlations are observed in peripheral (\(60\)-\(80\)%) Au+Au collisions which are very similar to the \(d\)+Au result indicating that correlations in peripheral Au+Au collisions can be considered as a superposition of independent \(p\)+\(p\) collisions. The away-side shape of associated particles at low \(p_{T}^{trig}\) evolves with centrality. The distribution becomes flat or slightly double-peaked for mid-central (\(20\)-\(40\)%) collisions. Fig. 3.2 shows azimuthal angle difference distributions in the \(0\)-\(12\)% central Au+Au collisions for different \(p_{T}^{assoc}\) and \(p_{T}^{trig}\) ranges.

In central Au\(+\)Au collisions, the away-side distribution is double-peaked for the lowest \(p_{T}^{trig}\) and the distribution becomes flatter with increasing \(p_{T}^{trig}\).

The away-side double-peak structure is not observed in the raw correlation shown in Fig. 3.1 and it appears after we subtract the elliptic flow background \(v_{2}\) using the ZYAM method. Recently it was suggested that triangular flow can be generated by hydrodynamic evolution of the collision system from event-by-event fluctuations of the initial collision geometry. Triangular flow results in a modulation of two-particle distributions in relative azimuthal angle of the form \(\cos 3\Delta\phi\), thus can contribute to the away-side double-peak structure. Background of triangular flow and higher order flow harmonics need to be subtracted. However, no first-principle physics can predetermine their relative strengths or (nontrivial) value boundaries [91] and order-by order subtractions of anisotropic backgrounds are challenging and suffer large uncertainties. We attempt to devise a novel method to subtract the anisotropic flow backgrounds using the data themselves. No assumption on the flow shape and amplitude are made and we are going to subtract all orders of flow background.

### 3.2 Methodology for two-particle azimuthal correlations

Fig. 3.3 shows a cartoon of the methodology. Di-hadron correlations are analyzed in two \(\eta\) regions symmetric about midrapidity, one (close-region) close to and the other

