#### 3.5.2 \(\eta\)-dependent efficiency correction

This analysis takes a difference between correlations from two separate \(\eta\) regions symmetric about midrapidity to subtract the anisotropic flow background. The single-particle \(\eta\) efficiency need to be corrected to have a tracking efficiency symmetric about midrapidity. The absolute efficiency is not corrected in the correlation measurements because this analysis deals with only the correlation shape, not the absolute amplitude.

Single-particle \(\eta\) distributions are histogramed as a function of \(z_{vtex}\) bin, centrality and particle transverse momentum and are normalized by the number of events. The \(z_{vtex}\) range of \(|z_{vtex}|<30~{}cm\) are divided into 15 bins with equal bin width. The \(\eta\)-dependent correction \(\epsilon_{\eta}\) (Eq. 3.4) is given by

\[\epsilon_{\eta}|_{z_{vtex},centrality,p_{T}}=\frac{\frac{dN}{d\eta}|_{z_{vtex}, centrality,p_{T}}}{\frac{dN}{d\eta}|_{symmetrized,|z_{vtex}|<2~{}cm,centrality,p_{T}}}~{}, \tag{3.4}\]

The \(\eta\)-dependent correction is obtained by treating symmetrized \(dN/d\eta\) distributions from \(z_{vtex}\) range \(|z_{vtex}|<2\) cm as the baseline. The baseline is obtained by taking the average of the two sides. The ratio of the \(dN/d\eta\) distribution from each \(z_{vtex}\) bin to this

