High-\(p_{T}\) particles (\(3<p_{T}^{\text{trig}}<10\) GeV/\(c\)) are selected with \(|\eta|<1\). Then we calculate the recoil transverse momentum \(P_{x}\) (Eq. 3.1) within \(-1<\eta<-0.5\) (\(P_{x}|_{-1}^{-0.5}\)) and \(-1<\eta<-0.5\) (\(P_{x}|_{0.5}^{1}\)) with respect to the high-\(p_{T}\) particle. If either of \(P_{x}\) (\(P_{x}|_{-1}^{-0.5}\) and \(P_{x}|_{0.5}^{1}\)) passes a cut of requiring a large recoil transverse momentum, this high \(p_{T}\) particle will be defined to be a trigger particle. The \(\eta\) ranges of close-region and far-region are defined with respect to \(P_{x}\). For \(P_{x}|_{-1}^{-0.5}\), the close-region is \(-0.5<\eta<0\) and the far-region is \(0<\eta<0.5\); for \(P_{x}|_{0.5}^{1}\), they are swapped.

The dihadron azimuthal correlation functions for close-region and far-region are given by

\[\frac{dn}{d\Delta\phi}=\frac{1}{N_{\text{trig}}}\cdot\frac{S(\Delta\phi)}{B( \Delta\phi)/B_{0}}~{}, \tag{3.5}\]

