### 4.6 Systematic Error Evaluation

In this analysis, we select events with a high-\(p_{T}\) particle (\(p_{T}^{trig}>3\) GeV/c) to do the three-particle azimuthal correlation. We divide the TPC \(\eta\) acceptance into two sections,

Figure 4.5: Background-subtracted three-particle correlations with \(3<p_{T}^{trig}<10\) GeV\(/c\) and \(2<p_{T}^{asso}<3\) GeV\(/c\) in \(60-80\)% peripheral Au+Au collisions. The red lines indicate the diagonal and off-diagonal and the black lines indicate the projection ranges.

one is \(-1<\eta<0\) and the other is \(0<\eta<1\). All tracks passing certain track quality cuts within the TPC acceptance are used. The systematic uncertainty is estimated by varying the track quality cuts, which is expected to be the main systematic uncertainty. Dca cuts are varied from less than \(2\;cm\) to \(1\;cm\) and \(3\;cm\). Nhitsfit cuts are varied from larger than \(20\) to \(15\), \(25\). Varying the \(\eta\) windows of the same-\(\eta\) region and cross-\(\eta\) region may be another source of the systematic uncertainties which is not included now.

### 4.7 Results and Discussion

We study the inter-jet correlations and intra-jet correlations by projecting the background subtracted three-particle correlations along the diagonal \(\Sigma\) and along the off-diagonal \(\Delta\), respectively.

* Off-diagonal projection: intra-jet correlations
* Diagonal projection: inter-jet correlations

The diagonal \(\Sigma\) (Eq. 4.10) and the off-diagonal \(\Delta\) (Eq. 4.11) are given by

\[\Sigma=(\Delta\phi_{1}+\Delta\phi_{2})/2-\pi\;\mbox{(away-side) or}\] \[\Sigma=(\Delta\phi_{1}+\Delta\phi_{2})/2\;\mbox{(near-side)}\;, \tag{4.10}\]

\[\Delta=(\Delta\phi_{1}-\Delta\phi_{2})/2\;, \tag{4.11}\]

Fig. 4.6 shows projections of near-side and away-side three-particle correlations along the diagonal \(\Sigma\) (Eq. 4.10) within \(0<\Sigma<0.35\) and along the off-diagonal \(\Delta\) (Eq. 4.11) within \(|\Sigma|<0.35\). The projection of the three-particle correlations is fitted by a Gaussian plus pedestal. We quantify the intra-jet or inter-jet correlation width by the Gaussian fit \(\sigma\).

Since we have finite binning and we are not projecting along one of the axes, bin width effects would appear in these projections along the diagonal or along the off-diagonal. This problem is solved by filling a two-dimensional histogram \(\Sigma\)-\(\Delta\) at the same time. Then we can project along the \(\Sigma\) (diagonal) or \(\Delta\) (off-diagonal).

Fig. 4.7 shows width of the diagonal and off-diagonal projections of the three-particle correlations.

Several physics conclusions can be drawn from the data.

* Near-side: The width of the near-side diagonal projection is larger than that of the off-diagonal projection.

High \(p_{T}^{trig}\) particles select preferentially dijets produced near the medium surface due to path length dependent energy loss. The near-side jets suffer minimal interactions with the medium and can be used to study parton fragmentation mechanisms. Partons fragment into a brunch of hadrons and obey the law of momentum conservation. Possible azimuthal distribution of hadrons with respect to the jet axis are shown in Fig. 4.8. The direction of a parton after initial hard scattering is defined as the jet axis. The left panel of Fig. 4.8 illustrates that the trigger particle and the two associated particles are on different sides of the jet axis. This results in a broadening of inter-jet correlations. While the right panel of Fig. 4.8 shows that the two associated particles are on different sides of the trigger particle. This results in a broadening of intra-jet correlations. Our data favor that there is a jet axis swing effect for the near-side jet. That is the trigger and the two associated particles are likely on different sides of the jet axis.
* Away-side: The width of the away-side diagonal is much larger than that of the off-diagonal projection (inter-jet correlation \(\gg\) intra-jet correlation). The away-side jet particles stay relatively together but their angles vary over a wide range event-by-event. One explanation is that the away-side jets are de

