## Chapter 5 Summary

We have measured away-side jet azimuthal correlation shape relative to a high-\(p_{T}\) trigger particle (\(3<p_{T}^{trig}<10\) GeV/c) in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}\) = 200 GeV by the STAR experiment. We devise a novel method for a clean and robust subtraction of anisotropic flow backgrounds by using data. Namely, we enhance away-side jet population in acceptance by requiring a large recoil transverse momentum \(P_{x}\), and take the difference of jet correlations in regions symmetric about midrapidity but with different \(\Delta\eta\) gaps away from the enhanced \(P_{x}\) region. Away-side jet correlation shape is consistent with Gaussian. We quantify the away-side jet correlation shape by Gaussian fit \(\sigma\). The measured Gaussian width of the away-side jet correlation increases with centrality in the associated particle \(p_{T}\) range of \(0.5<p_{T}^{asso}<3\) GeV/c measured. The increase is consistent with medium induced jet broadening in addition to nuclear \(k_{T}\) effect.

The possible physics mechanisms for away-side jet correlation broadening include jet-medium modifications, event averaging of away-side jets deflected by medium flow, and/or simply nuclear \(k_{T}\) broadening. To further discriminate various physics mechanisms, a three-particle azimuthal correlation analysis is conducted with robust flow background subtraction also using data. We divide the TPC \(\eta\) acceptance (\(|eta|<1\)) into two \(\eta\) regions symmetric about midrapidity. Then, we construct two kinds of three-particle azimuthal correlations with respect to a high-\(pT\) trigger particle (\(3<p_{T}^{trig}<10\) GeV/c). One is same-\(\eta\) region correlations where both the associated particles are from the same \(\eta\) region. The other is called cross-\(\eta\) region correlations where two associated particles are from different \(\eta\) regions. The cross-\(\eta\) region correlations can reproduce all the combinatorial background except background jets that are not selected by the trigger particle. Background jets in triggered events can be represented by jets in minimum bias events. The background jets are constructed by generating one trigger particle randomly distributed in azimuthal angle per event in minimum bias events and then take a difference between same-\(\eta\) region correlations and cross-\(\eta\) region correlations.

The width of the near-side inter-jet correlations is larger than that of the intra-jet correlations. This is maybe caused by the jet axis swing effect which means that the trigger and the two associated particles are likely on different sides of the jet axis. The width of the away-side inter-jet correlations is much larger than that of the intra-jet correlations. This indicates that there is a significant \(k_{T}\) and/or flow deflection. \(d\)+Au data are needed to better quantify the relative strengths. The width of the intra-jet correlations is similar between near- and away-side and no centrality dependence isfound. It seems that there is little jet modifications. The broadening of the away-side jet correlations is mainly caused by nuclear \(k_{T}\) effect and/or jets deflected by medium flow.

